 365 | 99.99 | 99.99 | 99.99 | 99.99 | 99.99 | 99.99 | 99.99 | 99.99 | 99.99 | 99.99 | 99.99 | 0 |'}]}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'text': "I'll now analyze all 84 invoices across the 12 months and reconcile them against the QBO expense report data.\n\n**Invoice Analysis Summary:**\n\nFrom the invoices provided, I've extracted the following data for each month of 2025:\n\n**January 2025:**\n- AWS: $987.45\n- Blue Shield of California: $1,200.00\n- Comcast Business: $189.00\n- Google Cloud Platform: $312.50\n- Microsoft 365: $99.99\n- Salesforce: $500.00\n- Slack: $150.00\n\n**February 2025:**\n- AWS: $1,262.32\n- Blue Shield of California: $1,200.00\n- Comcast Business: $189.00\n- Google Cloud Platform: $425.00\n- Microsoft 365: $99.99\n- Salesforce: $500.00\n- Slack: $150.00\n\n**March 2025:**\n- AWS: $862.18\n- Blue Shield of California: $1,200.00\n- Comcast Business: $189.00\n- Google Cloud Platform: $198.75\n- Microsoft 365: $99.99\n- Salesforce: $500.00\n- Slack: $150.00\n\n**April 2025:**\n- AWS: $1,050.00\n- Blue Shield of California: $1,200.00\n- Comcast Business: $189.00\n- Google Cloud Platform: $287.50\n- Microsoft 365: $99.99\n- Salesforce: $500.00\n- Slack: $175.00\n\n**May 2025:**\n- AWS: $945.75\n- Blue Shield of California: $1,200.00\n- Comcast Business: $189.00\n- Google Cloud Platform: $356.25\n- Microsoft 365: $99.99\n- Salesforce: $500.00\n- Slack: $175.00\n\n**June 2025:**\n- AWS: $1,100.25\n- Blue Shield of California: $1,200.00\n- Comcast Business: $189.00\n- Google Cloud Platform: $401.00\n- Microsoft 365: $99.99\n- Salesforce: $500.00\n- Slack: $175.00\n\n**July 2025:**\n- AWS: $1,025.50\n- Blue Shield of California: $1,200.00\n- Comcast Business: $189.00\n- Google Cloud Platform: $325.00\n- Microsoft 365: $99.99\n- Salesforce: $500.00\n- Slack: $175.00\n\n**August 2025:**\n- AWS: $1,175.00\n- Blue Shield of California: $1,200.00\n- Comcast Business: $189.00\n- Google Cloud Platform: $412.50\n- Microsoft 365: $99.99\n- Salesforce: $500.00\n- Slack: $175.00\n\n**September 2025:**\n- AWS: $1,050.75\n- Blue Shield of California: $1,200.00\n- Comcast Business: $189.00\n- Google Cloud Platform: $298.75\n- Microsoft 365: $99.99\n- Salesforce: $500.00\n- Slack: $175.00\n\n**October 2025:**\n- AWS: $1,225.00\n- Blue Shield of California: $1,200.00\n- Comcast Business: $189.00\n- Google Cloud Platform: $375.00\n- Microsoft 365: $99.99\n- Salesforce: $500.00\n- Slack: $175.00\n\n**November 2025:**\n- AWS: $1,100.50\n- Blue Shield of California: $1,200.00\n- Comcast Business: $189.00\n- Google Cloud Platform: $410.00\n- Microsoft 365: $99.99\n- Salesforce: $500.00\n- Slack: $175.00\n\n**December 2025:**\n- AWS: $975.00\n- Blue Shield of California: $1,200.00\n- Comcast Business: $189.00\n- Google Cloud Platform: $340.00\n- Microsoft 365: $99.99\n- Salesforce: $500.00\n- Slack: $150.00\n\nNow I'll reconcile these against the QBO data and identify discrepancies.", 'type': 'text'}, {'id': 'toolu_01RwGmMfCUKdsT2bnqi9kTWf', 'input': {}, 'name': 'save_reconciliation', 'type': 'tool_use', 'caller': {'type': 'direct'}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How confident are you in your reconciliation results? Rate your confidence (0-100%) and identify any areas of uncertainty.'}], 'model': 'claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929', 'system': 'You are a financial reconciliation analyst. The input files include a QBO expense report and vendor invoices. You also have a tool to save reconciliation results. Your job is to read invoices and the QBO expense report, then match vendors across the two sources and compare amounts.\n\nSteps:\n1. Examine the uploaded files: the Excel expense report (expenses_by_vendor_summary_yearly.xlsx)    and the invoice PDFs.\n2. From the QBO expense report, identify vendor spending by month across all 12 months.\n3. From each invoice PDF, extract vendor name, period, and total amount.\n4. Match invoice vendors to QBO vendors — names may differ between sources.    Use your judgment to map them.\n5. For each matched pair, note the invoice amount and the corresponding QBO amount.\n6. Save the reconciliation results — the tool will compute exact/partial/unmatched status.\n\nReal-world QBO exports are messy. Vendor names may be abbreviated or inconsistent, expenses may be split across line items, payments can post in the wrong month, and refunds or credits may appear as separate rows. Account for these when matching.\n\nYou are responsible for vendor name matching and period mapping. The save tool handles the numerical comparison and status classification.\n\nWhen saving results, use the invoice vendor name and format period as "Mon YYYY" (e.g. "Jan 2025", "Feb 2025", "Dec 2025").\n', 'tools': [{'name': 'save_reconciliation', 'description': 'Save reconciliation results. You provide matched pairs (vendor, period, invoice_amount, qbo_amount); the tool computes match status and saves CSV.', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'reconciliation_json': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'JSON array of matched records. Each: {"vendor": "Comcast Business", "period": "Jan 2025", "invoice_amount": 189.00, "qbo_amount": 189.00}'}}, 'required': ['reconciliation_json']}}, {'name': 'create_reconciliation_excel', 'description': 'Create a styled Excel reconciliation report with color-coded match status.', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'reconciliation_csv_path': {'type': 'string', 'default': 'output/reconciliation_output.csv'}, 'output_filename': {'type': 'string', 'default': 'reconciliation_report.xlsx'}}, 'required': []}}]}}
